<Project>

  <!--
    The single source of truth for the target-framework matrix.

    These lists exist here, rather than inline in each project, so that what the test matrix
    actually covers can be compared against what the library actually ships. Declaring them in
    one place is what makes a gap a build failure instead of a silent hole: they are baked into
    the test assembly as AssemblyMetadata and asserted against each other by RuntimeMatrixTests,
    so a runnable library target with no leg and no recorded reason fails the build.

    See ktsu-dev/JsonRequiredConditionally#30 for the regression that motivated this.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>

    <!--
      Library targets whose shared framework could host a test leg. Each binds a different in-box
      System.Text.Json, which is the whole reason a matrix is wanted here.
    -->
    <LibraryRunnableTargetFrameworks>net10.0;net9.0;net8.0;net7.0</LibraryRunnableTargetFrameworks>

    <!--
      Library targets that bind System.Text.Json from NuGet rather than from a shared framework.
      These cannot host a .NET (Core) test leg at all; reaching them needs a net472 leg, tracked
      in ktsu-dev/JsonRequiredConditionally#14.
    -->
    <LibraryPackageBoundTargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;netstandard2.1</LibraryPackageBoundTargetFrameworks>

    <!--
      The legs the test project actually builds and runs.

      These are exactly the runtimes CI installs — .github/workflows/dotnet.yml sets up 8.0.x,
      9.0.x and 10.x — so every leg named here can actually be launched there. A leg whose runtime
      is missing fails to launch outright rather than skipping, so this list and that setup-dotnet
      list have to move together.

      Three things had to clear before this could be more than net10.0, and all three have:

      1. Up to ktsu.Sdk 2.30.1, Sdk.targets cleared TargetFrameworks for every IsTestProject
         unconditionally, and is imported after both the project body and Directory.Build.targets,
         so a test project could not multi-target at all. 2.31.1 made that clear conditional on the
         value still being the SDK's own default, so an explicit override now survives.
      2. Building for more than one leg surfaced 204 latent analyzer violations (MSTEST0037,
         MSTEST0046, MSTEST0068) in pre-existing test files. Those are now fixed rather than
         suppressed. They are invisible on a single leg — the MSTest analyzers do not run there at
         all, even with the rules raised to error in .editorconfig — so the only thing that guards
         them is building the matrix, which CI now does.
      3. The test host's own dependencies put System.Text.Json 10 in every leg's output, which
         would have left the restored matrix cosmetic. See the test project for how that is
         handled, and for the two alternatives that were rejected.
    -->
    <TestTargetFrameworks>net10.0;net9.0;net8.0</TestTargetFrameworks>

    <!--
      Runnable library targets with no test leg. Every entry must carry its reason, because
      RuntimeMatrixTests requires each runnable target to be either covered or listed here.
      This list is the only way a gap can exist without failing the build.

      net7.0: blocked by MSTest.Sdk. MSTest.Sdk 4.x ships no net7.0 asset, so a net7.0 leg means
      restoring the MSTest.Sdk 3.11.1 pin that global.json's 4.4.1 replaced — a pin the whole
      repository would then build against, which is a maintainer's call rather than a detail of
      restoring the matrix. net7.0 is also out of support
      (EOL May 2024) and is no longer in ktsu.Sdk's own default target framework list, which is
      net10.0;net9.0;net8.0;netstandard2.0;netstandard2.1, while this library still ships a
      net7.0 asset. Whether to keep shipping it is a separate maintainer decision from whether
      it can be tested, so the asset is left alone and the gap recorded here. CI reflects the
      same picture: .github/workflows/dotnet.yml installs 8.0.x, 9.0.x and 10.x, but not 7.0.x.
    -->
    <UncoveredRunnableTargetFrameworks>net7.0</UncoveredRunnableTargetFrameworks>

  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
